            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AGoldschmidt) Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AWittekind) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(CA) Cahen (Belgium)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(D.V.H.) Dreyfus (avenue Victor Hugo)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EJHeilbrunn) Bruxelles, Belgique-The ERR removed Heilbrunn items from the warehouse of Continentale Menkès on successive dates: 24 February 1942, 16 April 1942, 17 March 1943, 2 November 1943.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(FvUnruh) Arcachon&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Hah) Hahn&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ILevy-Cerf) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JReder) Bruxelles, Belgique-24 works of art were removed from the Office des Propriétaires, 42, rue Wiertz, a storage company in Brussels. Other works were removed from 4, Square Marie Louise in Brussels. The Brüsseler Treuhandgesellschaft and the ERR were largely responsible for these thefts. Some of the items were transferred to the Jeu de Paume and were catalogued under the BN and FRE collection labels while others were sent to ERR HQ in Brussels, on rue du Chatelain.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(L.Mh.) Levy (Bd. Malesherbes)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LLevi) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MA-L) Möbel-Aktion Lack-Leder&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MBernheim) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MMDreyfus) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ORoth) Rotterdam, The Netherlands&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RBrunschwig) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Rouff) Chambord&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ST) Caroline Stern&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VeuveCLambert) Ixelles-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#3e2723'>
              <bucket>&quot;(WWurzberger) Forest-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(A Le) Arthur Levy&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Ad. W.) Adolphe Weiss--Native of Austria, Weiss had been the Portuguese consul in Vienna for 25 years prior to the Anschluss.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Arns) Hermann Arnstein-There is no information on Arnstein, bu we do know that he and his family were deported from France. There is no evidence that any of the Arnstein family members made it back from Nazi concentration camps. Some of the \&quot;recovered\&quot; Arnstein property were sold at auction by the French government.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ASS (12 H)) Assmances-This collection was one of the last to be processed at the Jeu de Paume in June 1944. Although the ERR specialists did not assign an owner to this set of confiscated objects at least two works in the ASS collection belonged to Alfred Lindenbaum (Lindon).&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(BLapage) Etterbeek-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(BRE) Bredel&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Cob) Coblentz&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(CReininghaus) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EBernheim) Bruxelles, Belgique-The US authorities dropped the claim on 28 June 1948.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EFabius) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(FHarburger) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Frederic) Jeu de Paume&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GMeyer) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GU) Friedrich Gutmann, Heemstede/Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HMLevy) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HValensi) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JHostie) Forest-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JLindauer) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(KOH) Kohnreich&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Kro) Joseph Kronig&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Levy) Mr. Levy-Blvd. Maurice Barres&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LID) LID&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(M.W.) Succession Max Wassermann&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MA-BUE) Möbel-Aktion Bucher&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Mannheimer, Vichy) Fritz Mannheimer, manager of the Dutch branch of the Berlin-based bank, Mendelssohn &amp; Cie, had residences in Amsterdam and Vaucresson, right outside of Paris. He spent his later years mostly in France and died in August 1939, right after getting married to his Brazilian-born nurse. Title to his collection was complicated by the fact that he placed most of the 3000 works and objects under his wife&apos;s name and used the bank&apos;s assets to acquire them through a British subsidiary called \&quot;Artistic\&quot;. This is one of the rare instances whereby the Reich leadership acquired a \&quot;confiscated\&quot; Jewish collection. The Mannheimer collections were plundered in Holland (1940-1941) and in France (1944).&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Marlio) Books&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MJacobson) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MLE) Maurice Leven&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Nizzaliste) ERR Südfrankreich 1944&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(OPPE) Oppenheim [Oppenheimer]&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(PMayer) Domaine de Bray, Seine Inf.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(PVera) Saint-Germain-en-Laye&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Roger Bernheim) rue Dosne&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Rosenberg Paris) Paul Rosenberg&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Si-ERF) According to postwar Belgian documents, this collection is of Belgian origin and is directly associated with the ERR in Belgium with no further information. The cards for Si-ERF were typed at Kogl.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Spira) Spira&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(UHDE) Uhde&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VAN) Vand&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Wbg) Alfred Weinberger&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#542437'>
              <bucket>&quot;(WLandowska) Saint-Leu-la-Foret&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(APozniak) Paris-Ms. Pozniak&apos;s collection consisted almost exclusively of works produced by Jewish artists active in interwar Paris, a number of whom were deported and died in Nazi camps.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Billy-Fenaille) Aveyron&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(CLE) van Cleef-The world-famous jeweler was based at Place Vendôme in Paris, one of the wealthiest Parisian enclaves, targeted by Nazis and local French collaborators for the immense wealth and high-end art that could be found there in private residences and in privately-owned galleries and luxury stores.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Duveen) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(FFF) ERR&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GLurcy) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HM) Hermann Milakowski&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JGoudstikker) Amsterdam&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(KLO) Klotz&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LEV) Levy (rue de Chezy)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(M.G.M. (11 X)) Michel Georges-Michel&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Man) Mandel&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MIC) Michelsohn&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Neuw/NWD) Neuwied Art objects were warehoused, sorted and catalogued at a customs house in Neuwied, Germany.  They came from the Netherlands, Belgium, Germany, and southern France. From there, they were transferred either to Berlin or to Schloss Kogl.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(PLLevy) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RM) Unknown&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(SEpstein) Ixelles-Bruxelles&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(TRosenthal) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#5c4033'>
              <bucket>&quot;(W) Georges Wildenstein&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(A.L.M.) A. L. Mayer&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Aro) Aronson (Schloss Brissac)-Naum Aronson was a well-established Jewish sculptor in interwar Paris.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(BN-Bruxelles) BN [Belgien und Nordfrankreich]--All items confiscated under the BN label were removed from the Brussels region. The ERR, the Brüsseler Treuhandgesellschaft (Belgium) and associated agencies performed these confiscations.  An unknown number of objects remained at ERR Headquarters in Brussels while the remainder was transferred to the Jeu de Paume in Paris.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Cohn) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ECassel) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(FJaval) Livry-sur-Seine&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GOL) Oskar Goldschmidt&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HOH) ERR collection&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JKaufmann) Bruxelles, Belgique-The Gestapo seized Ms. Kaufmann&apos;s belongings on 25 August 1942.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#692c3b'>
              <bucket>&quot;(KOT) Soma Koti&quot;</bucket>
